  "text": "Solution 1 is the most likely correct answer, though I could not verify it against the literature.\n\n- **Solution 1** gives q = 1 + Σₙ 1/(2ⁿ − 1) ≈ 2.6067, one plus the Erdős–Borwein constant. The derivation is heuristic, but the constant is specific and matches my recollection of the heavy-tailed divisible sandpile result (Hoffman, Johnson and Junge), which I believe states a threshold of about 2.6067. I can't confirm that recollection here.\n- **Solution 2** gives 1 + ln 2 ≈ 1.693. Its integral heuristic is plausible-sounding but unsupported, and I don't recognise 1 + ln 2 as the threshold.\n- **Solution 3** gives q = 2 from a volume-growth argument on ℤ. It only shows explosion on ℤ, but the question's threshold must hold uniformly over all bounded-degree graphs. Its claim that ℤ is the worst case is unjustified: combs and decorated graphs, which the other solutions consider, can force explosion at higher moments. So 2 is likely a lower bound, not the threshold.\n\nThe \"Final answer: 1\" line at the end of Solution 1 is a formatting artifact. The boxed value is 1 + Σₙ 1/(2ⁿ − 1) ≈ 2.6067.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
